ange in the way that people are  looking at their work  and their relationship with work  and i really believe this is going to  transform many industries in the  following years  so let's dig a little bit into why this  is happening people seem to be leaving  their jobs for three main reasons either  to move to a different company  or to change industries  or to start their own businesses in case  you're watching this video from the  future 2020  is the time when the pandemic started  and people were forced to work from home  with the amount of time people were  spending at home i think we started  looking at our lives in a different  perspective  and we really started reassessing our  values personally i really realize the  joy of spending time with friends and  family and the joy of giving time to  myself and just like doing little things  that nourish my soul and make me happy  and i think we also realize that life is  really short and for a few period of  months at the start of the pandemic we  had no freaking idea what was going to  happen  and i think people realize that life is  too short to be doing something that  doesn't bring us happiness or meaning  for a lot of people i guess that was  their jobs i think another side to this  is the slight detachment of work from  self-worth i think especially for  architects this is a bit of a pain point  because we  attach our values so intimately to our  work and you know the industry is kind  of designed that way  like  with the whole practice of judging a  person by their portfolio  um but i think in these past few years  we as we assessed our values  and um probably by having reduced  contact with society and societal  pressures  i feel like people are starting to  detach their value from their work i  have a colleague who recently left and  went to another firm  and when she told me where she was going  honestly i was a little bit shocked and  i thought it was a little bit strange  because i felt like it was a little bit  of a step down for her that firm wasn't  doing incredibly exciting work and it  was just like very conventional  architecture  but the more i thought about it it made  sense  she's getting better pay  better work-life balance and you know  that was her trade-off in the book  [ __ ] jobs by david graber  he talks about how the idea of  work as a source of value  is actually a pretty recent idea  and it really started with like  philosophers like john locke and  the puritan ideologies which basically  claimed that like suffering  endured by the working classes  is noble and virtuous that mindset has  been passed down to us  and on top of that in modern society our  jobs basically support our  very consumerist patterns of living  and we justify the daily dullness of  work because it fulfills our consumerist  desires  where we need to buy things we don't  really need  to climb the social ladder which in  itself is not real and i think that's  the other thing that's happened during  the pandemic our habits of consumption  have changed we're not really going out  as much we're not buying as much  and we don't need to spend as much  and all of a sudden you realize like  okay that's where all my money was going  the other reason is burnout  working from home blurred the lines  between people's work and personal lives  and a lot of people ended up working  more than there are a lot of number of  hours because for a lot of companies the  amount of work and deadlines didn't  change in fact a lot of the burden of  this shift to working from home fell on  the employees especially for visual  communicators like architects  in order to communicate effectively we  we had to produce more presentations and  more drawings but instead of having a  reduced workload a lot of that fell on  the employees to have to do the extra  work and put in the extra hours i think  really uh depending on how the firms  handled it this pandemic really exposed  a lot of the floss in the management of  the business also having to keep up  productivity while being isolated at  home is really hard because you're not  really getting the benefit of vibing off  of the energy of the office or your  colleagues which i think has led to  a questioning of the work culture  for me  culture is really important  it's a really important factor in my  work environment  i like having company outings and friday  night beers and building rapport with my  colleagues because  working with people that i like  helps me enjoy my work and it keeps me  invested in the company but the switch  to working from home  has really opened my eyes to how much  time i could save  and how much more productive i could be  from working from home  which directly gives me more time for  myself i think more and more people are  willing to trade that work culture for  better work-life balance  and for more control over their work  hours and schedules as the pandemic  starts to slow down some offices have  made it mandatory to go back to fully  working in the office but a lot of  employees have been really turned off by  this  because  we're i mean we've already demonstrated  for the past two years that we can work  independently and i think this just  shows a lot of it shows a lack of trust  and a lack of flexibility in the company  so i think this is gonna be a real  downside to companies that are enforcing  this the fourth reason is that we are in  the golden age of entrepreneurship  it's easier to start a business now than  ever since january to october this year  there were 4.7 million business  applications just in the us  which was a 34 increase from that same  time in 2019 first of all the cost of  failure is close to zero there's  multiple sources of capital that you  could tap into cost of testing out an  idea is super cheap you can literally  just ask a question to twitter or  facebook or instagram and with platforms  like fiverr and upwork it's so easy to  hire freelancers also there's